WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.669 --> 00:00:04.621
The Incredible Hulk is easily one of Marvel's
most recognizable characters—he's the big

00:00:04.621 --> 00:00:06.100
green guy with anger issues.

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:10.600
Now, Marvel Studios is beefing up Bruce Banner's
role in their cinematic universe with an arc

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:14.540
that's set to play out over the course of
three movies, starting in Thor: Ragnarok and

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:16.680
continuing through the next two Avengers films.

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:20.480
As for what shape that arc might take, that
remains to be seen—but based on what we

00:00:20.491 --> 00:00:22.990
know, we can certainly make a few guesses.

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:23.920
Some spoilers ahead.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:25.700
The story so far

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:29.740
If you're wondering why the Hulk's arc is
taking place in team-up pictures and other

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:33.520
heroes' movies instead of a standalone film,
it boils down to rights issues.

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:37.220
"As far as Hulk movies, standalone Hulk movies,
um…

00:00:37.229 --> 00:00:40.039
Marvel doesn't really have the rights to that
yet.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:41.400
That's still Universal's property."

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:45.420
But at the same time, it also raises the question
of whether they'd actually want to.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:50.760
Ang Lee's 2003 Hulk movie was—to put it
nicely—a bit of a mess, and despite pulling

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:55.030
off some interesting visuals that played with
comic book-style panel layouts, it's also

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:58.500
a movie that ends with a giant CGI monster
having a fistfight with a cloud.

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:03.879
2008's Incredible Hulk, starring Ed Norton
was equally messy, a half-reboot, half-sequel

00:01:03.879 --> 00:01:07.909
that, despite being the second film in Marvel's
shared universe, is also the one that nobody

00:01:07.909 --> 00:01:09.600
really remembers or talks about.

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:11.540
Enter the Avengers

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:15.520
When the Hulk showed up in 2012's The Avengers,
however, he was definitely memorable.

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:23.440
Maybe that's why he was the focus of so much
of the sequel.

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:27.240
Despite being loaded up with new characters,
Age of Ultron was as much a Hulk story as

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:28.600
it was an Avengers story.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:32.680
He's the one who gets the romance angle with
Black Widow, and who has the sad ending of

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:37.200
flying off to Parts Unknown in the Quinjet—kind
of an amped-up, superheroic take on the TV

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:38.260
show's traditional ending.

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:40.640
"(sad music)"

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:46.740
With all that in mind, it's easy to assume
that the Hulk might just work better in the

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:50.760
context of stories that feature other heroes—which
brings us back around to Ragnarok.

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:52.860
Ragnarok and Planet Hulk

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:57.399
According to Ruffalo, Thor: Ragnarok kicked
off his three-movie arc, and it's easy to

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:01.900
see where director Taika Waititi and Marvel
Studios got their starting point: Planet Hulk.

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:07.350
Published from 2006 to 2007, Planet Hulk tells
the story of Hulk being stranded on an alien

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:10.500
world that, for a little while at least, saps
him of his strength.

00:02:10.689 --> 00:02:14.640
He's forced to become a gladiator, and eventually
leads a revolution to overthrow the evil Red

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.860
King before returning to Earth and the next
phase of his adventures.

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:20.920
The entire middle section of Ragnarok pulls
heavily from this story.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:25.280
Characters like Korg and Miek, the planet
Sakaar and its brutal contests, and even Hulk's

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:28.940
Roman-inspired gladiatorial gear are lifted
directly from the comics.

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:33.300
The only big difference is that in the comics,
the Marvel superhero who shows up for a guest

00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:37.439
appearance as an opponent in the arena isn't
Thor—it's the Silver Surfer.

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:40.980
So if that's what we're working with now,
the question is, what's next?

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:42.740
Infinity War

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:47.120
It's been clear for a while now that the next
Avengers movie, Infinity War, will draw on

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:51.040
the events of Infinity Gauntlet, which still
stands as one of Marvel's greatest crossover

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:51.540
events.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:55.439
But while they've been seeding the Infinity
Gems throughout the MCU, the comic story they're

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:57.580
building on isn't actually all that Hulk-heavy.

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:01.019
That doesn't mean that the filmmakers couldn't
change things around and have the Hulk play

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:01.680
a bigger part.

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:05.520
In the same way Ragnarok advanced the Hulk's
story by combining Planet Hulk with pieces

00:03:05.629 --> 00:03:09.860
of Walt Simonson's epic mid-'80s run on Thor,
we might be seeing a movie that mashes up

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:12.980
Infinity Gauntlet with another Hulk story
to create something new.

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:15.200
We just need to figure out which one it might
be.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:17.080
World War Hulk

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.510
There's one pretty obvious follow-up to the
events of Planet Hulk.

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:25.000
In the comics, the events of that story led
directly to the next saga, World War Hulk,

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:29.560
in which the Hulk returned from Sakaar and
beat the dickens out of… well, everyone,

00:03:29.569 --> 00:03:32.320
who he blames for sending him out to Sakaar
in the first place.

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:33.980
So will Hulk turn on the other heroes?

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:37.600
Thanks to Ragnarok and the fight against the
Hulkbuster in Age of Ultron, we've already

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:41.239
seen how fun it can be to have the Hulk throw
down against the other Avengers.

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:44.860
But there's one problem with that story becoming
the inspiration for Hulks arc.

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:48.220
In the movies, Hulk isn't tricked—he boards
the Quinjet himself.

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:52.480
As we've seen in Ragnarok, the heroes of the
movies actually want Banner around, if only

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:55.080
so they can aim him at their strongest enemies
and let him go.

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:59.800
So as things stand, he doesn't have a reason
to be mad at the Avengers just yet.

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:00.300
Unless…

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:01.900
Days of Rage

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:06.780
One comic that might be a far more likely
bit of source material is "Days of Rage,"

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:10.500
a senses-shattering throwdown that took place
in Incredible Hulk #300.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:15.280
As the title implies, the basic idea of that
issue is that the Hulk goes into an unstoppable

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:20.200
rampage that finds him taking on the entire
roster of Avengers, plus Spider-Man and Doctor

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:20.700
Strange.

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:25.100
Even Daredevil, Luke Cage, and Iron Fist make
cameo appearances, although they're pretty

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:26.500
unlikely to show up in a movie.

00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:30.780
The centerpiece of the issue is a brutal fight
against Thor, and it ends with Strange whisking

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:34.650
the Hulk off into a cosmic adventure—exactly
the kind of thing that might get him involved

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:37.560
a space-faring battle centered on the Infinity
Gems.

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:39.420
Banner vs. Hulk

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:44.540
Toward the end of Ragnarok, Bruce Banner turns
back into the Hulk, presumably dooming himself

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:47.800
to a permanent life as a monster who can't
keep a pair of pants intact.

00:04:47.950 --> 00:04:52.060
But what if that's meant to set up an attempt
to "cure" Banner by separating him from the

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:52.820
Hulk once and for all?

00:04:53.020 --> 00:04:55.720
It's something that's been done in the comics
more than a few times.

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:59.880
While he serves as a two-fisted brick on the
team, Hulk's entire deal as a solo character

00:04:59.890 --> 00:05:02.610
revolves around a person dealing with himself.

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:06.390
Separating them would help to underline that,
and a purely raging Hulk would certainly provide

00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:07.960
a unique cinematic challenge.

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:09.780
The Maestro

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:14.190
Of course, if they really wanted to focus
on Bruce Banner's dark side, they could always

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:18.580
introduce the Maestro, an alternate future
version of the Hulk created by Peter David

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:20.440
and George Perez in Future Imperfect.

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:24.860
In that story, the core timeline's Banner—with
his mind in control of the Hulk's big green

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:29.310
body—heads off to an alternate future where
Maestro has ruthlessly conquered the world

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:32.760
and brutally dispatched every other superhero
in the Marvel universe.

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:36.620
Admittedly, while the X-Men franchise has
done, it, the MCU movies haven't really messed

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:38.380
around with alternate timelines yet.

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:41.300
That said, it's not actually out of the realm
of possibility.

00:05:41.420 --> 00:05:45.920
Evil opposites and dystopian futures are standard
tropes of the superhero genre, and since we're

00:05:45.930 --> 00:05:49.660
gearing up for a movie that's focused on a
set of rocks with the power to control time

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:51.600
and space, it could totally happen.

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:52.780
Thanks for watching!

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:54.960
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:57.080
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

